{
  "title": "Tune - Use RFT to Move to a Smaller Model",
  "locked": false,
  "description": "Tune Part B – Reinforcement Fine Tuning and model selection\n\nReinforce fine-tuning in Azure AI Foundry to improve agent decisions, shifting work from a larger model to a smaller one. We navigate the reward function, scenario-based training data, and evaluation results that demonstrate whether the tuned smaller model preserves quality while reducing cost.\n",
  "rows": [
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~20s",
      "narrative": "Our agent works, but it leans on a large model for its judgment. \n\nThat’s great for performance. But the ROI of our program is getting eroded. And we need to make changes to increase the ROI of AI. \n\nThe goal now: keep the decision quality and drop the cost. \n\nThat's reinforcement fine-tuning. \n",
      "demo_actions": "Frame the current architecture in Foundry as a working agent that currently relies on a larger model for decision quality. Set up this chapter as a cost reduction story: can we train a smaller model to make the same decisions well enough to take over more of the work?",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_081728_325_42.jpg"
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~30s",
      "narrative": "I have the flexibility to tune in multiple ways. \n\n\nBut when the task is making the right decision on something you can score cleanly — like an invoice call — RFT is the strong fit. \n",
      "demo_actions": "Open the Foundry fine-tuning overview. Highlight SFT, DPO, and RFT. Explain that this use case is about decision quality on a measurable task, which makes RFT a good method for training a smaller specialist model.",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_012438_tuning-options.png"
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~30s",
      "narrative": "This is the reward function — where we define a good decision in code. The model isn't rewarded for sounding polished. It's rewarded for choosing the correct action. ",
      "demo_actions": "Open the repo file for the reward or grader logic, such as rl/retail_grader_rft_tools.py. Introduce it as the mechanism that scores whether the smaller model is making the right business decisions.",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_081852_660_50.jpg"
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~30s",
      "narrative": "Essentially, this grader measures whether the cheaper model preserves the optimized expert behavior: grounded, cited contract-policy evidence with the right decision boundaries, rather than fluent but unsupported answers. \n",
      "demo_actions": "Scroll through the grader file and point out the business criteria it scores: correct decision, correct amount, correct tools, and correct workflow. Explain that this creates a measurable standard the smaller model can be trained against.",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_081852_660_50.jpg"
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~25s",
      "narrative": "The training data isn't generic chat. Each row is a real work scenario — the request, the policy context, the expected resolution, the tools, the actions. \n\nThat's how we teach the smaller model how Caldova actually operates. \n",
      "demo_actions": "Open the data JSONL files. Show that each record includes the user request, policy or system context, expected resolution, expected tools, expected actions, expected amounts, and scenario metadata.",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_082004_088_54.jpg"
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~25s",
      "narrative": "Once the reinforcement fine tuning job starts, we can monitor progress, see if its improving, and generally ensure that the process is proceeding well.\n",
      "demo_actions": "Show the console, eval output, or leaderboard. Compare a larger frontier model, a smaller untuned base model, and the fine-tuned smaller specialist on the same held-out set. Call out quality, tool use, task success, and cost.",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260703_124340_447_1.jpg"
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
